9. Required Fixes

Fix 1 – Do not allow one bad PDF to fail the entire ZIP

This is the primary functional fix.

The merge process should be changed so that PDFs are handled individually.

Expected behavior:

PDF 1 → valid → include
PDF 2 → valid → include
PDF 3 → invalid → skip + log
PDF 4 → valid → include
PDF 5 → valid → include
        ↓
Merge valid PDFs
        ↓
Generate final PDF

For the production case:

72170-4_1002036770_20250617.pdf

would be identified as non-mergeable and excluded from the final PDF, while the remaining valid PDFs continue through processing.

Important

The skipped PDF must be explicitly recorded in the logs so that operations/support can identify exactly what was excluded.

⸻

10. Fix 2 – Add Per-PDF Exception Handling During Merge

Currently the merge operation does not provide sufficient visibility into which source PDF caused the failure.

The implementation should add exception handling around each PDF’s merge operation.

The logging should capture:

* EPIC case/policy number
* source PDF filename
* source PDF path
* PDF index
* page count
* merge stage
* exception type
* complete exception/stack trace

Example expected logging concept:

PDF Merge - Processing file 4/6
File: 72170-4_1002036770_20250617.pdf
Pages: X
Merge failed
Exception: Invalid annotation dictionary
Action: Skipping PDF

This directly addresses Ashvi’s request for more detailed exception-level logging.

⸻

11. Fix 3 – Separate Merge Failure From ZIP Failure

The current outer processing treats an exception from PDF merge as a ZIP-level failure.

This should be changed.

Current behavior

PDF merge failure
      ↓
Exception propagated
      ↓
ZIP processing fails
      ↓
ZIP moved to unprocessed

Required behavior

PDF merge failure for one document
      ↓
Log problematic PDF
      ↓
Skip problematic PDF
      ↓
Continue with remaining PDFs
      ↓
Generate final PDF
      ↓
Continue normal delivery

The ZIP should only be considered failed if no usable PDFs remain or if a critical processing step itself fails.

⸻

12. Fix 4 – Preserve Information About Skipped PDFs

The existing logging already has:

Valid PDF File Count
Skipped PDFs During Merge Count
Skipped PDFs During Merge

This should be extended so that skipped files are explicitly captured with the reason.

For example:

Valid PDFs: 4
Skipped PDFs: 1
Skipped:
72170-4_1002036770_20250617.pdf
Reason: Invalid annotation dictionary during Aspose merge

This will make production troubleshooting much easier.

⸻

13. Fix 5 – Add Pre-Merge PDF Validation

Before performing the final merge, each transformed PDF should be validated for merge compatibility.

The validation should specifically cover:

* PDF can be opened successfully by the configured Aspose version.
* PDF pages can be accessed.
* PDF can be processed without an invalid annotation/object dictionary.
* PDF can be incorporated into the merge operation.

If validation fails:

PDF marked as non-mergeable
        ↓
Logged
        ↓
Excluded from final merge
        ↓
Remaining PDFs processed

This is preferable to discovering the bad document only after the entire merge operation has already started.

⸻

14. Fix 6 – Review and Upgrade Aspose PDF Version

As specifically requested by Ashvi, the current Aspose PDF library version must be reviewed.

The team should:

1. Identify the exact Aspose PDF version currently used in the application.
2. Check whether the version is outdated.
3. Review Aspose release notes/issues related to:
    * annotation dictionaries
    * PDF page merging
    * invalid PDF dictionaries
    * PDF annotations
    * PDF save/serialization
4. Move to the latest approved/compatible Aspose version if applicable.
5. Regression-test the EPIC PDF workflow after the upgrade.

Important RCA wording

Do not state:

“Upgrading Aspose will fix the issue.”

Instead:

“The current Aspose version will be reviewed and, where an approved version contains relevant PDF annotation/merge fixes, upgraded as part of the remediation.”

This keeps the RCA technically accurate.

