18. RCA Conclusion

The production failure was caused by an Aspose PDF merge incompatibility with an annotation dictionary contained in one of the source EPIC PDFs. The individual PDFs were successfully generated, but the problematic document caused the final Aspose merge operation to fail with An attempt to create annotation using invalid dictionary. For the investigated EPIC case 76696817, the problematic document was identified as 72170-4_1002036770_20250617.pdf; excluding this document allowed the remaining documents to generate the final PDF successfully.

The remediation will therefore focus on isolating and logging individual PDF merge failures, excluding only non-mergeable PDFs, allowing the remaining valid documents to continue through final PDF generation, and reviewing/upgrading the approved Aspose PDF version and annotation handling. This will prevent a single malformed/unsupported PDF from causing the entire ZIP processing flow to fail.

⸻

19. Implementation Changes – Short Summary for the Jira/POC

You can put this as a table at the end of the document:

Area	Change
PDF Merge	Process/validate PDFs individually so one bad PDF does not fail the entire merge
Exception Handling	Catch merge exceptions at individual PDF level
Logging	Log filename, index, page count, stage, exception and stack trace
Skipped PDFs	Maintain skipped-PDF list with exact reason
ZIP Processing	Continue ZIP processing when only a non-critical PDF fails
Final PDF	Generate final PDF using successfully processed PDFs
Annotation Handling	Investigate/handle malformed or unsupported annotation dictionaries
Aspose	Review current version and upgrade to approved version if relevant fixes are available
Error Messages	Replace generic merge error with document/stage-specific messages
Testing	Test normal PDFs, problematic production PDF, multiple PDFs, blank-page insertion and zero-valid-PDF scenario

One important implementation principle

Don’t simply catch the exception and continue using the same partially modified mergedDoc. The merge design should isolate the failing PDF before it can corrupt/fail the overall merge state—ideally through a validation/pre-merge step or an isolated merge attempt, followed by merging only the validated PDFs.

That is the key technical point I would carry into the code implementation.
